        ":::\n",
        "  \n",
        "A strong correlation tells us that two variables move together. It does **not**, by itself, establish that one variable causes the other.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Build the MLR Model\n",
        "  \n",
        "Now let's move from **exploration** to **modelling**.\n",
        "  \n",
        "Suppose we want to explain cement sales using the five explanatory variables:\n",
        "  \n",
        "$$\n",
        "Sales_t =\n",
        "\\beta_0 +\n",
        "\\beta_1 Advertising_t +\n",
        "\\beta_2 Housing_t +\n",
        "\\beta_3 Price_t +\n",
        "\\beta_4 Rainfall_t +\n",
        "\\beta_5 GDPGrowth_t +\n",
        "\\epsilon_t\n",
        "$$\n",
        "  \n",
        "Our question is:\n",
        "  \n",
        "> **Do these variables contain useful information for explaining cement sales?**    \n",
        "  \n",
        ":::{.callout-tip title=\"💻Coding Session\"}"
      ],
      "id": "88f5d978"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "#| output: true\n",
        "#| label: lst-prog_6\n",
        "#| lst-cap: Multiple Linear Regression Model\n",
        "\n",
        "import statsmodels.api as sm\n",
        "\n",
        "# Define dependent and independent variables\n",
        "y = df[\"Cement_Sales_000_Tons\"]\n",
        "\n",
        "X = df[\n",
        "    [\n",
        "        \"Advertising_Rs_Lakh\",\n",
        "        \"Housing_Starts_000_Units\",\n",
        "        \"Average_Cement_Price_Rs_Bag\",\n",
        "        \"Rainfall_mm\",\n",
        "        \"GDP_Growth_Percent\"\n",
        "    ]\n",
        "]\n",
        "\n",
        "# Add intercept\n",
        "X = sm.add_constant(X)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Fit MLR model\n",
        "model = sm.OLS(y, X).fit()\n",
        "\n",
        "# Display regression results\n",
        "print(model.summary())"
      ],
      "id": "lst-prog_6",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
